WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.500
Not another Rattata.

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:09.300
So the carrier T-Mobile
is offering a free data, or zero-rated,

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:12.780
deal for the popular Pokémon Go social
game.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:16.619
Some critics have said that this
violates the FCC's net neutrality rules.

00:00:16.619 --> 00:00:20.730
They allege that T-Mobile is somehow
picking winners by choosing to zero-rate

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:22.320
this particular game.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:26.039
Well, the fact of the matter is that zero-rating and free data are not violations

00:00:26.039 --> 00:00:28.050
of the FCC's net neutrality rules.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:31.890
The FCC looks at zero-rating on a
case-by-case basis,

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:35.569
but if it made a flat-out ban on zero-rating, it would likely violate the First

00:00:35.579 --> 00:00:38.280
Amendment and the freedom of speech. But more
than that

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:42.210
T-Mobile is not picking the winners at
all. This is perhaps the most popular video

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:45.930
game of all time. It's been downloaded on more mobile phones than any. They're simply

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:49.350
responding to what their particular
users want and hoping that they can win

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:52.900
new customers by taking advantage of a
marketing opportunity.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:56.550
People don't value all content the same,
so the idea that every piece of data

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:59.640
should be priced the same doesn't really
make any sense.

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:03.390
If you'd ban zero-rating out of the
gate, you'd have a lot fewer carriers

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:07.000
because they're be very few ways to
differentiate in the marketplace.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.549
What do you think about T-Mobile's
Pokémon Go deal?

00:01:11.549 --> 00:01:14.460
Let us know in the comments section
below, and let us know what other

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:17.729
questions you'd like AEI scholars to answer in 60 seconds.

